abx-args-of = What A and B differ in; A is always what the settings say
abx-args-against-restoration = B's lossy restoration under --of restoration [default: extend, or off where the settings restore]
abx-args-against-quality = B's resampler quality under --of resampler [default: low-latency, or very-high where the settings say low-latency]
abx-args-against-filter-phase = B's resampler phase under --of resampler [default: the settings' own]
abx-args-against-dither = B's dither under --of dither [default: none, or triangular where the settings dither none]
abx-args-against-noise-shaping = B's noise shaping under --of dither [default: the settings' own]
the-player = A high-fidelity music player
cli-config = Read settings from FILE instead of config.toml in the settings folder
cli-library = Use the library database FILE, which is created where it is not there
cli-vault = Keep the managed vault at DIR
cli-sink = Target the sink with this node.name
cli-quality = Resampler quality
cli-filter-phase = The resampler's phase: linear rings before a transient as well as after it, minimum only after it, intermediate between the two
cli-dither = Which dither covers a word length the output is too short to hold
cli-noise-shaping = Which curve the dither noise is shaped by. threshold is designed at the output rate and shapes at every rate; lipshitz runs at 44.1 and 48 kHz and is flat elsewhere
cli-bit-perfect = Switch the graph to the source's rate for this run, whatever the bit-perfect setting says
cli-no-bit-perfect = Stay on whatever rate the graph is already running at, rather than switching it to match the source. Trades bit-accuracy for never interrupting another client
cli-files = Files to queue in the window this opens, each a path or the file:// URI a desktop entry's %U passes
transport-args-shuffle = Play the queue in a shuffled order, starting anywhere in it
transport-args-repeat = Repeat nothing, the track playing or the whole queue
transport-args-volume = Start at this volume, 0 to 100, rather than the volume setting's
party-wanted-start = Begin a party, playing these to everyone who joins
party-wanted-join = Join a party by its link or code, hearing each song from your own copy
ytmusic-args-keep = Keep a song or a playlist from a YouTube Music link: queue it and fetch what is queued into the music folder, one song at a time. A song already held is not fetched again. Needs youtube-music on, and yt-dlp
ytmusic-args-check = Say whether the account reaches Premium formats, by asking yt-dlp about one album song
ytmusic-args-sign-in = Sign in to YouTube Music in a window of its own (resonate-signin), keep the account's cookies in the data folder, point youtube-music-cookies at them and check the account. Needs youtube-music on
ytmusic-args-sign-out = Take away what the sign-in window kept, its cookies and its browser profile, and clear youtube-music-cookies where it names them
shared-args-share = Share this playlist and print a link to edit it with and one to follow it by
shared-args-join = Join the playlist someone shared by its link
shared-args-leave = Leave this playlist; its songs stay here as a playlist of your own
shared-args-remove-person = Take this person off the playlist --from names; everyone else moves to new links
shared-args-from = The playlist --remove-person takes someone off
shared-args-reset = Make new links for this playlist, so the old ones reach nothing
shared-args-name = The name other people see beside you
sync-args-join = Join the devices whose pairing code this is, leaving the ones this device synced with
sync-args-leave = Leave the devices this one synchronises with; it starts afresh with a new code
sync-args-forget = Take a device that is away off every device's list; one that syncs again comes back
sync-args-fetch = Fetch the songs of this playlist this device lacks from the device --from names
sync-args-from = The device --fetch takes songs from
sync-args-handoff = Play the queue this device was last playing, from the song it was on
sync-args-control = Show what this device plays in its zones, or drive it with --press, --seek, --set-volume, --jump, --remove or --move
sync-args-zone = The zone of the --control device to show or drive, by number or name; the first unless given
sync-args-press = Press a transport key on the --control device's zone
sync-args-seek = Go to this place in the song the --control device's zone plays: seconds, 1:30 or 1m30s
sync-args-set-volume = Set the volume of the --control device's zone, 0 to 100
sync-args-jump = Play this row of the --control device's queue, 1 the first
sync-args-remove = Take this row out of the --control device's queue, 1 the first
sync-args-move-row = Move this row of the --control device's queue to the row --to names
sync-args-to = Where --move puts the row, 1 the first
scan-args-full = Read every file again, rather than only those whose size or time has moved since the last scan
scan-args-follow-links = Walk into symbolic links to files and folders; a folder reached twice is walked once
playlist-args-add = Append these files to the playlist instead of playing it, creating it if it is new
playlist-args-export = Write the playlist out instead of playing it, as M3U, PLS or XSPF by the file's extension
playlist-args-tidy = Drop every row whose file is no longer on disk, instead of playing it
playlist-args-fold = Drop every row naming a file an earlier row already names, keeping the first of each, instead of playing it
playlist-args-drop = Take the rows --matching names out of the playlist, instead of playing it. The rows it does not match stay, the files stay where they are, and there is no --drop without a search, because a playlist holding nothing is one to discard
playlist-args-into = Copy this playlist's rows into OTHER instead of playing it, creating OTHER if it is new. The rows stay here too, --matching copies only the ones that text matches, and a playlist cannot be copied into itself
playlist-args-order = Put the rows in this order, instead of playing it. A row no scan has seen goes to the end, except under file, which reads the path every row carries
playlist-args-reverse = Put them in that order the other way round, whichever of --order and --sort named it
playlist-args-keep = Keep it in that order from now on, so a row added later lands where the order puts it rather than at the end
playlist-args-by-hand = Stop keeping it in order and leave the rows where they stand, so a row added later lands at the end again
playlist-args-query = Start a playlist that fills itself with whatever this text matches, rather than holding a fixed list, or change the search one already named fills itself from. Give it empty to match the whole library. Words match a title, artist or album, and terms like artist:"pink floyd", year:1970-1979, added:<30d, length:>5m, rate:>=96k, depth:24, codec:flac and is:lossless narrow what they match. plays:>5 counts every play a track ever had and plays:>5@30d only the ones inside that span. A leading - takes one out, and or between two means either will do
playlist-args-sort = The order a saved query hands its rows back in
playlist-args-limit = Stop a saved query after this many rows
playlist-args-matching = Play only the rows this text matches, in the order the playlist holds them, or copy only those where --into names another playlist, or take only those out where --drop is given. It reads the way a saved query's own text does, and a row no scan has seen matches nothing
playlist-args-rename = Rename the playlist instead of playing it. A name is one name however it is written, so a spelling another playlist already holds is refused
playlist-args-discard = Discard the playlist and every row in it instead of playing it. The files stay where they are, and the command line has no undo to put it back with
playlist-args-pin = Pin the playlist instead of playing it, so it stands at the top of the listing and of the window's sidebar
playlist-args-unpin = Unpin the playlist instead of playing it, so it takes its place in the order the listing is read in
playlist-args-refresh = Read the playlist again from the link it was imported from, instead of playing it. One kept in step takes the service's rows and order, dropping what the service took out; one imported once only gains what it lacks. Songs the library does not hold are wanted, so a poll fetches them
playlist-args-upkeep = Say how the playlist follows the link it was imported from, instead of playing it: once leaves it as it stands, in-step reads the link again every six hours while the window is open, and whenever `resonate playlists --refresh` runs
playlist-args-forget-link = Forget the link the playlist was imported from, instead of playing it. Its rows stay, and it is never read from the link again
queue-args-files = Files to queue, each a path or a file:// URI
queue-args-playlist = Queue the rows of this playlist rather than files named here, reading them out of the library this build holds and sending one a call. A playlist that fills itself from a search is queued as whatever it matches now
queue-args-player = Queue onto this player, by the bus name `resonate players` prints or by the instance under it. With none it is the one answering to the plain name, or the lowest instance where another process holds that
queue-args-zone = Address this zone of the window by its number (1 is the first) or by the name the window shows. With neither this nor --player it is the first zone
queue-args-next = Play them straight after the row being played, ahead of anything already queued, rather than after what is queued
queue-args-play = Play the first of them as it lands, rather than leaving the transport on the row it is on
queue-args-move-to = Queue nothing: move what the zone --zone names plays (its song, where it stands, its queue and whether it plays) onto this zone of the window, by its number or its name. The other zone opens its own device and fades in from where the first was heard while the first fades out and stops
vault-args-import = Re-encode every scanned track the vault does not already hold, strip its tags and its pictures, validate what came back and point the catalog at it. Nothing is written until --apply says so
vault-args-roots = Import or release only the tracks scanned from this library root. Repeat it to name more than one; with none, every root is taken
vault-args-at-most = Import at most N tracks in this run
vault-args-apply = Make the import, the release, the prune or the compaction rather than printing it; after --verify, mend what did not read back
vault-args-verify = Decode every object and cover the vault holds and weigh what comes back against what went in. With --apply, point each track of an object that did not read back at its own file, to be weighed again by the next import, and let every album go of a cover that did not
vault-args-prune = Count the objects no track and no album names any more, and with --apply take them away
vault-args-release = Count the vaulted tracks whose own file is still there, and with --apply point each back at that file. A track the vault holds the only copy of stays in it, and the objects released are left for --prune to take away
vault-args-compact = Count the JPEG covers an earlier build kept as they came that a byte-exact JXL transcode would hold in less room, and with --apply swap each for its transcode, proved to give the very JPEG back, and point its albums at it
eq-args-pub = Which device's binding the rest of this command reads or writes, by the node.name `resonate sinks` prints. With none it is the binding every device that names none of its own falls back to. With --import, --fetch or --fit-measurement the profile kept is bound to it
eq-args-on = Turn the equaliser on. It is a filter, so the stream stops being bit-perfect and `resonate explain` says so
eq-args-off = Turn the equaliser off for every device and leave the samples untouched. Every binding stays, so --on puts them back, and one device is let go with --unbind
eq-args-profile = Bind this kept profile to the device --for names and turn the equaliser on. With --import, --fetch or --fit-measurement it names what the new profile is kept as, and with --export it names which one is written out
eq-args-unbind = Take the binding away from the device --for names, or from the fallback where it names none
eq-args-import = Read a profile in and keep it. An EqualizerAPO ParametricEQ file is read as the bands it names; an AutoEq GraphicEQ file is kept as its curve and fitted onto third-octave bands at the rate each stream plays at
eq-args-fit-measurement = Fit a correction to a frequency-response measurement of your own and keep it as a profile: AutoEq's CSV, REW's exported text or two columns of frequency and decibels. Both are set level at the middle of the spectrum, smoothed to a sixth of an octave, and up to ten bands, shelves among them, are fitted to what lies between the measurement and --target. Named by --profile or by the file, bound to the device --for names, or kept as its own curve with --own
eq-args-measure = Measure the output and the microphone yourself and fit a correction to what comes back, as --fit-measurement fits a file: a logarithmic sweep from 20 Hz to 20 kHz is played through the output --sink (or the configured or default one), straight to the device and past the equaliser, while a microphone records it, and the recording is deconvolved to a frequency response. Quiet is best, and the microphone should sit where the head would. Kept as a profile named by --profile, else `measured`, bound to the device --for names, or kept as its own curve with --own
eq-args-microphone = Record --measure with this microphone, by the node.name `resonate listen --microphones` prints, or the default one where none is named. With the flag absent it is `listen-from`, which is the desktop's own monitor unless set: that measures the signal path to the output, not a room or a transducer
eq-args-keep-measurement = Also write what --measure measured as a CSV of frequency and decibels, which --fit-measurement reads again
eq-args-calibration = The microphone's own response, in the formats a measurement is read from (the calibration file a measurement microphone comes with is REW's), taken off what --measure heard so the fit corrects the output and not the microphone. Without it the microphone is taken to be flat
eq-args-target = What --fit-measurement or --measure fits toward: flat, harman-over-ear-2018 or harman-in-ear-2019, or a file in the same formats as a measurement. With none it is harman-over-ear-2018
eq-args-export = Write a profile out as an EqualizerAPO ParametricEQ file — the one --profile names, or the one bound to the device --for names
eq-args-find = List the measured devices whose name holds every one of these words, with who measured each and on what rig. Nothing is fetched and nothing is kept
eq-args-fetch = Fetch the correction measured for this device and keep it, named by --profile or by the device. It is the path --find prints
eq-args-suggest = Name the measured device the chosen sink looks like, or say that nothing answers to it clearly enough to be worth guessing at
eq-args-fetch-every = Fetch the correction every measurer made for the devices whose name holds every one of these words, at most as many as --find lists, and keep each as `<device> (<measurer>)`, so the same headphones from several measurers can be tried against one another. A device the source holds no correction for is said and passed over
eq-args-list = List the profiles kept here, with how many bands each holds and which devices each is bound to
eq-args-measurer = Prefer the correction this measurer made (oratory1990, crinacle, Rtings and so on, any case) beside --find or --suggest: its row comes first, or is the one suggested, where it measured the device, and the usual ranking of measurers stands where it did not
eq-args-forget = Discard a kept profile and unbind every device bound to it. The file is taken away, and the command line has no undo to put it back with
eq-args-own = Bind the device --for names to a curve of its own and turn the equaliser on. With --import, --fetch or --fit-measurement what is read becomes that curve rather than a kept profile, and with --export that curve is what is written out, bound or not
eq-args-forget-own = Discard the curve of its own the device --for names holds, or every other device's where it names none, and take the binding away where it was bound to that curve. It is how a device gone for good stops leaving its curve behind
favourites-args-tracks = List the favourite tracks and nothing else
favourites-args-albums = List the favourite albums and nothing else
favourites-args-artists = List the favourite artists and nothing else
favourites-args-add = Mark the track, album or artist of this name a favourite, whichever of --tracks,                 --albums and --artists says it is. Repeat it for several
favourites-args-remove = Take the track, album or artist of this name off the favourites. Repeat it for                 several
favourites-args-by = Narrow a track or album named by --add or --remove to this artist's, where                 several share the name
wants-args-add = Want the release track of this title that the catalog holds no file for, as the                 window's Missing pane does. It lists the same ones `missing` does
wants-args-remove = Stop wanting the track of this title; a delivery already kept stays
wants-args-artist = Narrow --add or --remove to the tracks of this artist
wants-args-album = Narrow --add or --remove to the tracks of the album of this title
browsing-args-artist = List the albums of the artist the service knows by this id
browsing-args-album = List the songs of the album the service knows by this id
browsing-args-mix = List the songs of the mix the service knows by this id
browsing-args-search = List the artists, albums and songs the service finds for these words
browsing-args-favour = Mark the song the service knows by this id as a favourite in the service itself
browsing-args-unfavour = Take the song the service knows by this id out of the service's own favourites
spotify-args-follow = Keep the liked songs and every playlist of the signed-in account in step, each a playlist here, and want every song the library lacks
spotify-args-move-once = Copy the liked songs and every playlist of the signed-in account here once, and follow none of them
spotify-args-forget = Forget the refresh token and stop following the account; its playlists keep their rows here
served-args-service = The service to browse: subsonic, jellyfin, tidal, resonate, hifi-api, monochrome or ytmusic; without it, the first the settings set
sub-sinks = Print the sinks the sound system advertises, and the rates it will switch to
sub-sinks-renderers = Search the local network for UPnP media renderers and Google Cast receivers too and list them beside the sound server's sinks, whatever the renderers key says
sub-sinks-renderers-at = Reach the UPnP media renderer whose description is at this http address, sending no search to the network unless --renderers asks for one: for a network that drops multicast; repeatable, and the renderers-at key holds addresses for every run
sub-sinks-cast-at = Reach the Google Cast receiver at this IP address or host name (port 8009 unless one is given), sending no search to the network unless --renderers asks for one: for a network that drops multicast; repeatable, and the cast-at key holds addresses for every run
sub-scan = Scan library roots and exit. With no paths, rescans every registered root
sub-roots = Print the library roots a bare `scan` will walk
sub-enrich = Ask the reference about every album and artist not asked lately, and keep what it answers
sub-enrich-refresh = Ask again about albums and artists already answered
sub-enrich-albums = Ask about at most N albums and N artists this run
sub-wants = List the release tracks marked wanted, with what a provider has delivered, or want one or stop wanting it
sub-sync = Show this device's pairing code and the devices it synchronises with; join another's devices by their code, fetch a playlist's songs from one or play what one was playing
sub-shared = List the playlists shared with other people; share one, join one by its link, leave one, or take someone off one
sub-party = Listen together: begin a party others join by a link, or join someone's, each person hearing their own copy of the song
sub-missing = List the release tracks the catalog holds no file for, and the releases of held artists the catalog holds none of
sub-missing-artist = List only the tracks and releases of the artist with this name, or narrow --dismiss and --dismiss-release to them
sub-missing-read-the-rest = First ask MusicBrainz for the releases past the first thousand the artist is credited on
sub-missing-bring-back = First list again every track and release dismissed from the window's Missing pane
sub-missing-dismiss = Dismiss the missing track of this title from the Missing pane, and list nothing; --bring-back lists it again
sub-missing-dismiss-release = Dismiss the release of this title that a held artist has and the catalog does not, and list nothing
sub-missing-album = Narrow --dismiss to the tracks of the album of this title
sub-musicbrainz = The copy of MusicBrainz kept on this device: say what is held, build it from the newest export (downloaded, about 7.5 GB) or from a folder holding one, remove it, or search it. Lookups ask it before the network while musicbrainz-mirror is on
sub-musicbrainz-build = Download the newest export and build the copy from it, replacing any held
sub-musicbrainz-from = Build from mbdump.tar.bz2 and mbdump-derived.tar.bz2 in this folder rather than downloading them
sub-musicbrainz-remove = Remove the copy and what was downloaded for it
sub-musicbrainz-search = Search the copy for recordings by these words
sub-tidal = Sign in to TIDAL: print the link and the code to approve it with, wait for the approval and keep the refresh token in the settings file. Needs tidal-client-id set
sub-jellyfin = Sign in to the Jellyfin server jellyfin names with Quick Connect: print a code to approve in a Jellyfin app already signed in, wait for it and keep the session in the settings file in place of the password
sub-jellyfin-forget = Forget the kept session instead
sub-spotify = Sign in to Spotify with an app you registered at developer.spotify.com: print the address to approve it at, wait for the approval and keep the refresh token in the settings file. Needs spotify-client-id set, and the app listing http://127.0.0.1:43879/spotify as a redirect URI
sub-ytmusic = Keep songs from YouTube Music, or check the account yt-dlp reaches it through
sub-lastfm = Sign in to Last.fm so what is heard is scrobbled there: print the page to allow Resonate on, open it where a browser can, wait for the allowing and keep the session key in the settings file. Needs lastfm-key and lastfm-secret, an API account's
sub-lastfm-forget = Forget the session key, so nothing more is scrobbled to Last.fm
sub-poll = Ask every registered provider for each wanted track not tried lately
sub-poll-again = Ask about the wanted tracks tried lately as well
sub-forget = Drop library roots, and every track scanned from them; a track a provider delivered, named by the path or URI `resonate wants` lists it under; or, for a folder inside a root, every track under it whose file is gone, even from a drive that is not mounted
sub-tag = Print the tags that would be written into every scanned file to say what the catalog was told about it. Nothing is written until --apply says so
sub-tag-root = Write into the files scanned from this library root alone. Repeat it to name more than one; with none, every root is written
sub-tag-apply = Write the tags rather than printing them. Each file is read back afterwards and the catalog follows what it now says
sub-tag-undo = Put back what the last applied run replaced in every file it wrote, printed until --apply writes it: each field as it read before, a field the run added taken away, a cover it added taken out and the rating it changed restored
sub-dedupe = Print every file holding the same bytes as another, such as Song (1).flac beside Song.flac or a song copied under a new name, with the copy to fold into the song kept. Nothing is folded until --apply says so
sub-dedupe-apply = Fold each copy into the song it copies: its plays, favourite, playlist entries and queue rows move to the song, and the copy goes to the trash. A file whose bytes differ is left where it is
sub-vault = Say what the managed vault holds. The vault keeps a bit-exact copy of every track imported into it, stripped of its tags and re-encoded, and the library it was imported from is read and never touched
sub-organise = Print the moves that would file every scanned track under the layout the organise-as setting names. Nothing is moved until --apply says so
sub-organise-layout = File the tracks under this layout rather than the one the organise-as setting names, for this run alone
sub-organise-root = File only the tracks scanned from this library root. Repeat it to name more than one; with none, every root is filed
sub-organise-apply = Make the moves rather than printing them. Every file is renamed where it stands, the catalog follows it, a file beside it sharing its name travels with it, and a folder the moves leave empty is taken away
sub-organise-undo = Put back what the last applied run moved, every file to where it stood before, the catalog following. Printed until --apply says so, and walking it back twice files them again
sub-play = Play files through the engine and print what the transport does
sub-queue = Queue files or a playlist onto a player already running, through the interface it puts on the session bus
sub-players = Print the players of this build answering on the session bus, with what each is playing
sub-served = Browse a service the settings set to play in place (Subsonic, Jellyfin or Emby, TIDAL, the Resonate server, hifi-api, Monochrome): its first page (a Subsonic server's artists, a TIDAL account's favourites), an artist's albums, an album's songs or what a search finds, each with the URI play and queue take to stream it from there
sub-subsonic = Browse the Subsonic server the settings name (subsonic, subsonic-user, subsonic-password), as served --service subsonic does
sub-playlists = Print the playlists the library holds
sub-playlists-order = List them in this order, newest or most played first for all but the name
sub-playlists-reverse = Turn the listing around from the way that order reads, oldest or least first
sub-playlists-named = List only the playlists whose name holds every one of these words. A playlist has only a name to answer with, so terms like year: and is: are passed over
sub-playlists-refresh = Read every playlist kept in step with a link (Spotify, Deezer, Apple Music, TIDAL, YouTube, ListenBrainz) from it again now, instead of listing them, and want the songs the library does not hold
sub-playlist = Play a playlist, add files to it, copy it into another, drop what a search matched, save or change a query, write it out, put it in order or keep it in one, tidy it or fold its doubles
sub-eq = Print the equaliser in force, turn it on or off, bind profiles to devices, keep and fetch them, and find the device a correction was measured for
sub-abx = Run a blind listening test, level-matched, over one song looped, keys on standard input: the equaliser bound to the output in use against a bypass of itself, or the restoration, resampler or dither in force against another
sub-abx-trials = Hold this many trials, from 1 to 100
sub-import = Read playlists in from M3U, PLS or XSPF files, appending to one already named, from a link to a playlist on Spotify, Deezer, Apple Music, TIDAL, YouTube or ListenBrainz, or from a public Deezer profile's link (every playlist it made and its loved tracks), once or kept in step with it
sub-import-files = A playlist file, or a link to a playlist on a service. A link's songs the library holds fill the playlist, and the rest are wanted, so a poll fetches them
sub-import-upkeep = How a playlist read from a link follows it: once reads it now and appends to a playlist of that name, in-step makes a playlist of its own that takes the service's rows and order every time the link is read again (every six hours while the window is open, or `resonate playlists --refresh`). A profile in step is followed whole, its new playlists taken and its deleted ones let go; once moves every playlist to Resonate and stops following it
sub-import-name = Name the playlist this, rather than taking the name the file declares
sub-sleep = Set the sleep timer on a player already running, and print what it reads back as
sub-sleep-player = Set it on this player, by the bus name `resonate players` prints or by the instance under it. With none it is the one answering to the plain name, or the lowest instance where another process holds that
sub-sleep-zone = Set it on this zone of the window, by its number (1 is the first) or by the name the window shows. With neither this nor --player it is the first zone
sub-stats = Print what the catalog has counted: how much was played, and what was listened to most
sub-stats-window = Count only the plays inside this span
sub-stats-top = List this many tracks, albums and artists in each table
sub-update = Ask now whether a newer release of Resonate is out, and print it beside this build's version and the page it is on. The window asks at most once a day while check-for-updates is on
sub-wrapped = Print Resonate Wrapped: a year of what was listened to, as the window's slides read
sub-wrapped-year = The year to review; without it, last year until December and this year from then on
sub-wrapped-summary = Print the short summary a message can carry, and nothing else
sub-favourites = Print the tracks, albums and artists marked a favourite
sub-suggest = Print the playlists the catalog suggests making out of what it holds, with what each would fill itself from
sub-suggest-save = Save the suggestion of this name as a playlist that fills itself from the search behind it
sub-share = Print the link a track is shared as
sub-share-file = The file to write a share for. With none, it is whatever the player already running is playing
sub-mcp = Serve the catalog and the running player to a language model over the Model Context Protocol, as newline-delimited JSON-RPC on stdin and stdout
sub-mcp-player = Reach this player, by the bus name `resonate players` prints or by the instance under it. With none it is the one answering to the plain name, or the lowest instance where another process holds that
sub-explain = Print the output plan for a file against the selected sink
sub-info = Print everything known about a file: tags, ReplayGain, container layout, bitrate
sub-info-graph = Draw the bitrate over time
sub-analyse = Decode a file end to end and say whether its lossless claim holds, how loud it is and what its audio prints as
sub-analyse-file = A file, a file:// URI whose #frames=START-END names one cut of it, or a .cue sheet with --track
sub-analyse-track = The track of the .cue sheet to analyse, by the number the sheet gives it
sub-analyse-recognise = Ask the recognition service what the audio is. Needs `acoustid-key` in config.toml
sub-listen = Listen to what the desktop is playing, or to a microphone, and name the song — in the library or not
sub-listen-microphone = Listen to a microphone rather than the desktop: the one of that node.name, or the default where none is named
sub-listen-seconds = How long to listen for, from 4 to 60 seconds; twelve is what the signature is taken over
sub-listen-microphones = List the microphones there are to listen to, and listen to nothing
sub-studies = List what the studies the lookup takes of every track found: the fakes, the suspects and the tracks whose audio is another song
sub-studies-fakes = Only the tracks judged fake: a lossy transcode, an upsample or padded bits
sub-studies-suspects = Only the tracks whose spectrum makes them suspect
sub-studies-misnamed = Only the tracks whose audio was recognised as another song
sub-studies-take = Name a track by what its audio was recognised as: the title, the artist and the recording, in place of what its file says. A file, or a file:// URI whose #frames=START-END names one cut of it
sleep-spec-means = How much longer to play: a bare number of minutes, a length such as 30m, 1h30m or 1:30:00, track to stop at the end of the one playing, queue to stop at the end of the queue, or off to take the timer away
